<break time="600ms"/>
the mom call drill.
<break time="600ms"/>
if talking to your mother (or another high-trigger person) leaves you anxious, prep like it’s an exam:
<break time="600ms"/>
set a limit: i’ll stay on the call for … minutes.
<break time="600ms"/>
choose 3 safe topics: …
<break time="600ms"/>
one boundary statement: “i don’t want to talk about that right now.”
<break time="600ms"/>
exit strategy: “i have to go, we’ll talk later.”
<break time="600ms"/>
post-call self-care: …
<break time="600ms"/>
when did you realize you were parenting your partner instead of being loved by them?
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" /> take a moment. pause here, write what comes up, and come back when you're ready. <break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
what would it feel like to be loved without earning it?
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" /> pause here. there's no rush. press play when you're ready to continue. <break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
make two lists of friends.
<break time="600ms"/>
who drains you, and who actually shows up for you?
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" /> stop the audio for a moment and write your answer. come back whenever you're ready. <break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
practicing boundaries in friendships next time you feel yourself slipping into “mom friend mode,”
<break time="600ms"/>
pause and try:
<break time="600ms"/>
listening without fixing.
<break time="600ms"/>
saying: “that sounds tough, what do you think you’ll do?”
<break time="600ms"/>
noticing your urge to rescue . without acting on it.
<break time="600ms"/>
how did it feel to step back and just be a friend?
<break time="600ms"/>
building supportive relationships take stock of your closest people (family, partners, friends).
<break time="600ms"/>
circle the ones who: let you vent, respect your needs, offer help without strings.
<break time="600ms"/>
star the ones who: only come when they need something, leave you drained after interactions.
<break time="600ms"/>
the “no” practice.
<break time="600ms"/>
this week, practice saying:
<break time="600ms"/>
“i can’t help with that right now.”
<break time="600ms"/>
“no, i’m not available.”
<break time="600ms"/>
“that doesn’t work for me.”
<break time="600ms"/>
write down moments where you said “no” to a partner or friend when normally you would have said “yes.”
<break time="600ms"/>
how did they respond? how did i feel?
<break time="600ms"/>
remember this...
<break time="600ms"/>
being loved isn’t the same as being needed. boundaries don’t push people away: they show you who is capable of loving you as you are. the more you practice them, the more you’ll attract relationships where you’re cared for, not just leaned on.
<break time="600ms"/>
in this chapter, you’ve looked at how relationships drain you when boundaries are missing: but for many eldest daughters, the biggest leak isn’t time or energy. it’s money.
<break time="600ms"/>
this next chapter is about reclaiming that power. because money isn’t just money: it’s your future.
</speak>
